From 070e9c30c8a5da2b9b4bc85057025799f598ede1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 04:44:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): OFD lock guard on every board connection;
 stranded readers reopen [daveey]
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Problem: Kanban processes (Desktop backend, TUI gateways) were stranded on a
deleted kanban.db-wal/-shm generation. Every Kanban read then failed with
"disk I/O error" or reported false corruption until a restart, and the
dashboard quarantined (copied) the healthy board about 10 times a minute
(self-heal kanban-759104eb56, three episodes on 2026-10-27 on hermes-hub;
the 03:49Z episode on 10-28 also damaged the live board).

Why: POSIX advisory locks belong to the process. Any plain open()/close() of
the db or -shm inside a process cancels every lock SQLite holds there
(sqlite.org/howtocorrupt §2.2). The next sibling process to close the board
then thinks it is the last user, checkpoints and unlinks -wal/-shm. state.db
has been guarded against this since Sept (hermes_state_lockguard: OFD locks
on SQLite's own descriptors, which a stray close cannot cancel). Kanban
never got the guard, and ~17 of its openers were raw sqlite3.connect().

Fix:
- hermes_cli/kanban_lockguard.py: connect() is a drop-in sqlite3.connect
  that returns a guarded connection (GuardedConnection, composable via
  guarded_factory with connect_tracked's tracking close). guard()
  re-applies the guard; unguard() is needed before leaving WAL.
  stranded_sidecars() and shm_identity() detect a stranded process. Only
  WAL databases are guarded, and the guard is lifted before the handle's
  own close, so a true last close still ends the generation.
- Every hermes_cli/kanban*.py board opener goes through it, including
  kanban_db_connect (tracked opens, the fenced ro path, and re-guard after
  WAL activation), session_activity (_open_ro, cached_ro and the read
  helpers), notify, origin, sync_client, chat_links, awaiting, wait_check,
  capability_registry, origin_snapshot, workspace and dispatch_owner.
- cached_ro reopens a cached reader whose -shm generation is gone. It closes
  the stale connection before reopening, because a new connection would
  share SQLite's per-inode WAL-index mapping.
- (The dashboard init_db-per-request loop was fixed separately on main in
  f2d0e23785, so it is not part of this change.)

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_lockguard.py. On the old code, 9 of 10
fail, including open_ro, cached_ro and kbc.connect: "a sibling's close
deleted -wal under a live connection". A lint-style test keeps raw
sqlite3.connect out of kanban*.py. Two existing tests adapted to the new
contract:
test_write_txn_check_reads_correct_header_fields unguards before leaving
WAL, and test_wal_lock_guard_lifecycle checks only its own inodes in the
shared handle table.
